PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

All five of the items are used by Chaucer as rhyme-ends in the *Book of the Duchess*. The poem is written in octosyllabic couplets and the lexemes turn up as normal rhyme words in the course of the 1,334 lines:

* wente – e.g. the –ente couplet set that is repeatedly used for the past of *go/wend* in the dream narration;
* here – the –ere rhyme class *here / there / were / dere* etc. is a regular Chaucerian rhyme;
* fool – the –ool class, *fool / cool / pool* etc.;
* hool – the Middle English form of *whole/hull* is used in the same –ool class, and in the Duchess it is rhymed with *fool/cool* etc.;
* countour – the ME *countour / tournour* –our class is attested in the poem.

Hence none of A–E is a word that Chaucer fails to rhyme in the *Book of the Duchess*; the correct choice is None of the above.

